make-doc xc!+ xc xc-addr1 -- xc-addr2  ) xchar	x-c-store
Stores the xchar @var{xc} at @var{xc-addr1}. @var{xc-addr2} is the
next unused address in the buffer.  Note that this writes up to 4
bytes, so you need at least 3 bytes of padding after the end of the
buffer to avoid overwriting useful data if you only check the
address against the end of the buffer.

#line 63 "kernel/xchars.fs"
make-doc xc!+? xc xc-addr1 u1 -- xc-addr2 u2 f  ) xchar x-c-store-plus-query
Stores the xchar @var{xc} into the buffer starting at address
@var{xc-addr1}, @var{u1} chars large. @var{xc-addr2} points to the
first memory location after @var{xc}, @var{u2} is the remaining
size of the buffer. If the xchar @var{xc} did fit into the buffer,
@var{f} is true, otherwise @var{f} is false, and @var{xc-addr2}
@var{u2} equal @var{xc-addr1} @var{u1}. XC!+?  is safe against buffer
overflows, and therefore preferred over XC!+.

