#line 87 "kernel/xchars.fs"
make-doc -trailing-garbage xc-addr u1 -- xc-addr u2  ) xchar-ext minus-trailing-garbage
@i{xc-addr1 u1} is a string of @i{u1} chars.  @i{u2} is the size of
the string after removing the chars from the end that do not
constitute a complete, valid xchar.@*The idea here is that if you
read a fixed number of chars, e.g., with @word{read-file}, there
may be an incomplete xchar at the end; you eliminate that with
@word{-trainling-garbage}, leaving a valid xchar string for
processing (if the string starts with a complete xchar and only
contains valid xchars).  You prepend the eliminated chars to the
next read block of chars so you do not miss any parts.

#line 98 "kernel/xchars.fs"
