@i{xc} is the xchar starting at @var{xc-addr1}.  @var{xc-addr2 u2}
is the remaining string behind @var{xc}.  If the start of
@i{xc-addr1 u1} contains no valid xchar, @i{xc} is
@word{invalid-char}, and @i{xc-addr2 u2} is the remaining string
after skipping at least one byte.  If @i{u1}=0, the current
behaviour does not make much sense and may change in the future:
@i{xc-addr2}=@i{xc-addr1}+1, @i{u2}=MAX-U, and @i{xc} is either 0
or @word{invalid-char}.

